NANO12, FNANO13, FNANO14, FNANO15, FNANO16, FNANO17, FNANO18, FNANO19, FNANO20, FNANO21,FNANO22 and FNANO23).  The 21st Conference on Foundations of Nanoscience (FNANO24) will have a mixture of invited talks by distinguished scientists as well as contributed posters and open discussion periods to enhance attendee interaction with the goal of creating a vibrant intellectual community in the areas of nanoscience and self-assembly.  Nanoscience Prize Award: Each year, the ISNSCE Nanoscience Prize is awarded at FNANO to recognize and encourage outstanding research in all areas of nanoscience. Special tracks introduce topics of emerging interest to the self-assembly community. The Robert Dirks Molecular Programming Prize: The Dirks Prize recognizes exceptional early-career achievement by a researcher working in any area of molecular programming, whether theory, experiment, computation, or a combination thereof. These prizes will be awarded during distinct evening sessions of the upcoming FNANO24 conference.   PRIOR FNANO CONFERENCES & SCHEDULES\nNUMBER FNANOX SCHEDULE\n0th FNANO03 FNANO03schedule\n1st FNANO04 FNANO04schedule\n2nd FNANO05 FNANO05schedule\n3rd FNANO06 FNANO06schedule\n4th FNANO07 FNANO07schedule\n5th FNANO08 FNANO08schedule\n6th FNANO09 FNANO09schedule\n7th FNANO10 FNANO10schedule\n8th FNANO11 FNANO11schedule\n9th FNANO12 FNANO12schedule\n10th FNANO13 FNANO13schedule\n11th FNANO14 FNANO14schedule\n12th FNANO15 FNANO15schedule\n13th FNANO16 FNANO16schedule\n14th FNANO17 FNANO17schedule\n15th FNANO18 FNANO18schedule\n16th FNANO19 FNANO19schedule\n17th FNANO20 FNANO20schedule\n18th FNANO21 FNANO21schedule\n19th FNANO22 FNANO22schedule\n20th FNANO23 FNANO23schedule\n    \n    Foundations of Nanoscience: Self-Assembled Architectures and Devices\n    John H. Reif (reif@cs.duke.edu), Hao Yan (hao.yan@asu.edu)  and Jacob M. Majikes (jacob.majikes@nist.gov), Conference co-Chairs and\n    Andrew Turberfield (a.turberfield@physics.ox.ac.uk), Program Chair\n    Copyright 2024--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJohn H. Reif (https://users.cs.duke.edu/~reif/conferences.html)\nciteturn37search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; General Chairman of FNANO18, Snowbird, Utah, April 16-19, 2018. ... Program Chairman of FNANO07, Snowbird, Utah, April 18 -21, 2007.\n\nJohn H. Reif\n\nConference Organization:\n\n(FNANO = FOUNDATIONS OF NANOSCIENCE: SELF-ASSEMBLED ARCHITECTURES AND DEVICES)\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO24, Snowbird, Utah, April 28-May 1, 2024.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO23, Snowbird, Utah, April 14-17, 2023.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO19, Snowbird, Utah, April 15-18, 2019.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO18, Snowbird, Utah, April 16-19, 2018.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO17, Snowbird, Utah, April 10-13, 2017.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO16, Snowbird, Utah, April 11-15, 2016.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO15, Snowbird, Utah, April 13-16, 2015.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO14, Snowbird, Utah, April 14-17, 2014.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO13, Snowbird, Utah, April 15-18, 2013.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO12, Snowbird, Utah, April 16-19, 2012.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO11, Snowbird, Utah, April 11-15, 2011.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO10, Snowbird, Utah, April 27-30, 2010.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO09, Snowbird, Utah, April 20-24, 2009.\n\nGeneral Chairman of FNANO08, Snowbird, Utah, April 22 -25, 2008.\n\nProgram Chairman of FNANO07, Snowbird, Utah, April 18 -21, 2007.\n\nProgram Chairman of FNANO06, Snowbird, Utah, April 23-27, 2006.\n\nProgram Chairman (with coChair John Monahan) of NSF Workshop: Emerging Opportunities of Nanoscience to Energy Conversion and Storage, Arlington VI, November 21-22, 2005 (See Workshop Report)\n\nProgram Chairman of FNANO05, Snowbird, Utah, April 24-28, 2005.\n\nProgram Chairman of FNANO04, Snowbird, Utah, April 21-23, 2004.\n\nProgram coChair man (with Chen), The 9th International Meeting on DNA Based Computers, Madison, Wisconsin, June 1-4, 2003.\n\nProgram Chairman of 34th ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing(STOC2002), Montréal, Québec, Canada, May 19-21, 2002.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Evolution of DNA-Based Molecular Computing | Springer Nature Link (https://springerlink.fh-diploma.de/chapter/10.1007/978-981-19-9891-1_3)\nciteturn37search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; However, circuits across multiple origamis via inter-origami communication have not been realized.\nCircuit size in each protocell and communication efficiency between protocells remain to be explored.\n\nThe addressability of DNA origami at nanometer precision facilitated the confinement of molecule reactions on DNA origami surface, which enables templated separation of reactions on different origamis (Fig. 2c). In 2017, Chatterjee et al. proposed spatialized information propagation by using DNA origami as the canvas to design circuits [33]. The circuit on DNA origami receives input signals and fuel strands from the solution and releases output strands for readout, allowing signal communication between origamis. However, circuits across multiple origamis via inter-origami communication have not been realized. Due to the lack of threshold and amplifier functions in such spatial-separated systems, this strategy still faces challenges when increasing scale of the circuit. DNA self-assembly may provide alternative solutions to construct localized response elements with noise suppression and signal amplification functions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFNMA - Conference on Functional and Nanostructured Materials | FTiMS - Politechnika Gdańska (https://ftims.pg.edu.pl/ptf-og/fnma-conference-functional-and-nanostructured-materials)\nciteturn37search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago;   * The 12th Conference on Functional and Nanostructured Materials FNMA’15, 6–10 September 2015, Zakynthos I