\nSubmission deadline December 19, 2025\nTopics: nanotechnology proteins self assembly nucleic acid  FNANO 2026: Abstract Submission Closed Foundations of Nanoscience 2026, 23rd Annual Conference. Mon. March 16 to Wed. March 18, 2026. Technical University of Munich, Downtown Campus, Arcisstrasse 21, 80333 Munich, Germany Dear colleagues, Thank you very much for the overwhelming interest in FNANO 2026. We have received an exceptionally large number of abstract submissions in a very short time, which is both gratifying and a strong indication of the vitality of the FNANO community. As announced on the FNANO 2026 homepage, the total number of abstracts was limited to 130 due to capacity constraints set by the planned conference facilities in Munich. This limit has now been reached, and abstract submission has therefore been closed earlier than the extended submission deadline. Unfortunately, this means that not all interested contributors were able to submit in time, and I sincerely apologize to those who were affected by this. I hope for your understanding in this situation. We are very grateful for the strong engagement and enthusiasm, and we will do our utmost to assemble an exciting and high-quality program that reflects the breadth and excellence of the submitted work. I very much look forward to welcoming you to FNANO 2026 in Munich and hope to see many of you there. With best regards, Kurt V. Gothelf Program Chair FNANO 2026 Professor at the Department of Chemistry and iNANO, Aarhus University, Denmark kvg@chem.au.dk  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProgram Structure and Requirements (https://users.cs.duke.edu/~reif/GPNANO/GPNANO.html)\nciteturn37search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Program Courses and Faculty Database URL: http://www.cs.duke.edu/nano/ ... A number of graduate programs in nanoscience have also been established, for example a Professional Masters Program in Nanoscale Physics at Rice University (www.profms.rice.edu), and a nanotechnology graduate program at University of Washington (www.nano.washington.edu/education). ... Examples of ongoing fundamental nanoscience research at Duke include: electron transport in carbon nanotubes and DNA lattices (CHEM, CS, PHY), theory and simulation of spin dynamics in quantum dots (CHEM, PHY), optical properties of semiconductor quantum dots (ECE, PHY), studies of friction at the nanoscale (MEMS), and nanoscopic aspects of protein-folding (BIOCHEM). ... 2) Synthesis (Jie Liu, Dept of Chemistry & other faculty) - covers key principles and methods of chemistry and materials science that allow the creation of a variety of nanostructures (e.g., Carbon nanotubes and quantum dots). ... Also demonstration of brass or plastic molecular models for Crystallographic Model Building.\nMethods: Molecular biology, chemistry, microscopy (AFM, TEM, SEM, STM, etc.)\n\nDNA-Based Computing. Principles and Historic Development\n\nDNA-Based Nanofabrication. Self-Assembling DNA Tilings as Structural Templates\n\nMolecular Electronics\n\nBioChips -- Surface Based Chemistry (DNA and Protein Chips)\n\n \n\n* ECE2xx: Nanoelectronics (Dept of Electrical and Computer Engineering)\n\nInstructors: April Brown, Richard Fair, and Hisham Massoud\n\nDescription: The course will cover materials and devices for nano-scale electronic circuits.\n\n \n\n* CPS 222: Nanocomputers (Dept of Computer Science)\n\nInstructors: Lebeck\n\nDescription: Design and analysis of nano-scale computing devices. Topics include nanoelectronic devices (e.g., carbon nanotube transistors, quantum cellular automata, etc.), computational paradigms, component design, defect and fault tolerance, fabrication techniques (e.g., self-assemblies), modeling and simulation methods.\n\n \n\n* CPS296.x : Biomolecular Nanotechnology (Dept of Computer Science)\n\nInstructors: Thom LaBean\n# = Core Faculty of the Nanoscience Program\n\n* = Member of the Nanoscien